00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:35,120
This three part series is based on a recording that I found in the Internet

122
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:40,640
archive that has. there's really no way for me to give it any better attribution

123
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:43,200
than it There it is. It was just kind of sitting there.

124
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:46,680
Everything that I can figure out with this recording indicates that it was

125
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:49,160
in the late 1980s. It's an interview

126
00:07:49,160 --> 00:07:55,280
A scout is conducting with Green Bar Bill and he has told us the story

127
00:07:55,280 --> 00:08:00,240
of how he became a scout before there was all of this organizational

128
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:04,680
stuff happening in scouting, because he got a copy of Scouting for Boys

129
00:08:04,720 --> 00:08:09,640
in 1910. He put a patrol together and part one of this explains exactly

130
00:08:09,640 --> 00:08:12,920
how that all happened. He traveled to the United States.

131
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:17,520
He became involved with the Boy Scouts of America, and he ended up

132
00:08:17,840 --> 00:08:20,120
being the voice of the Boy Scouts of America.

133
00:08:20,200 --> 00:08:24,680
He had written all of the major reference materials for the Boy Scouts

134
00:08:24,680 --> 00:08:27,840
of America based on his understanding of scouting.

135
00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:32,799
This source code idea is not to turn back the clock

136
00:08:33,280 --> 00:08:38,319
and not to make us a bunch of stodgy old traditionalists who are

137
00:08:38,679 --> 00:08:43,039
basically just conducting, you know, some kind of historic

138
00:08:43,039 --> 00:08:47,319
reenactment of the first Brown Sea Island encampment with

139
00:08:47,319 --> 00:08:49,599
Baden Powell. That's not our aim,

140
00:08:49,599 --> 00:08:51,079
That's not what we're trying to do.

141
00:08:51,079 --> 00:08:55,400
But what were the principles underlying the whole idea that

142
00:08:55,599 --> 00:09:01,120
Bill took on when he was a boy, at age 10, and then elucidated

143
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:04,360
in all the writing and all the work that he did over his lifetime?

144
00:09:04,440 --> 00:09:06,040
Why is it still relevant today?

145
00:09:06,040 --> 00:09:09,440
We're a century later, you know. why is it still relevant?

146
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:13,640
Because if we agree that it's relevant and we get a hold of

147
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:15,640
that source code, that's pretty powerful.

148
00:09:15,800 --> 00:09:19,040
That's something that we can really take and run with right

149
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:22,960
To introduce today's installment of this story

